# Price war or quiet collusion?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why do two firms competing on price drive it to cost, but two competing on quantity do not? When do cartels hold, and when do they break?"
next: {"title":"Should big tech be broken up?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/41-should-big-tech-be-broken-up.cast"}

## A price war on the quay
vars:
    pa:
      value: 70
      min: 0
      max: 80
    pb:
      value: 70
      min: 0
      max: 80

    scale ladder x 100 y 220 width 800 label "$ per crate" min 0 max 80 value 10 ticks 8 markers [{"value":70,"label":"one boat"}] hidden true
    icon boat_a size 80 set twemoji x 800 y 535 or ["boat"] icon_look picture of sailboat bind.x "100 + 10*pa" hidden true
    text tag_a "A: ${pa:0}" #b5482e x 800 y 598 font_size 26 bind.x "100 + 10*pa" hidden true
    icon boat_b size 80 set twemoji x 800 y 415 or ["boat"] icon_look picture of sailboat bind.x "100 + 10*pb" hidden true
    text tag_b "B: ${pb:0}" #2f6b8f x 800 y 478 font_size 26 bind.x "100 + 10*pb" hidden true

    card title "Price war or quiet collusion?"

Two firms, one market: will they fight a price war, or settle quietly into high prices? Take two fishing boats at Harbourtown's quay.
    draw ladder

B: Say, with illustrative numbers, each crate costs ten dollars to catch, and both holds are full.
    text cost_tag "cost $10" #8f887c x 200 y 150 font_size 24

    pause 0.3

One boat alone would charge seventy dollars a crate. That's the monopoly price, where it earns the most.
    draw boat_a tag_a

B: Now both boats chalk a price on the quay. The restaurants buy from whichever is cheaper: same fish, same quay.
    draw boat_b tag_b

B: My bet is they share the buyers and stay near seventy. Why spoil a good thing?
    highlight boat_a boat_b

    ask "Where does the price end up with two boats? Click on the line." on ladder store g default "50" tolerance 0.08
        right: Yes: you said {g} dollars, and it ends at {g.true}, the cost. Here is why.
        wrong: You said {g} dollars; it ends at {g.true}, the cost. Here is why.

    erase ladder_answer

At sixty-nine dollars, A doesn't get half th (@highlight boat_a tag_a@)e buyers. It gets all of them.
    animate pa 69 duration 1.2

B: So B answers with sixty-eight. Th (@animate pa 67 duration 1@)en A with sixty-seven, and down it goes.
    animate pb 68 duration 1

It stops only at ten dollars, the cost. Below that, every crate sold loses money.
    animate pa 10 pb 10 duration 3.5 easing linear

    text big10 "$10" #b5482e x 360 y 480 font_size 64

    pause 0.5

B: Two boats, and the price is where a thousand boats would put it. Joseph Bertrand pointed this out in 1883.
    portrait bertrand x 860 y 560 width 170 of "Joseph Bertrand"

It's called the Bertrand paradox. It sounds too strong, and it is, once you change one thing.
    highlight big10 effect circle

    erase bertrand

## Compete on catch instead
domain: {"x":[0,130],"y":[0,140]}
vars:
    qa:
      value: 60
      min: 0
      max: 90
      step: 1
    P:
      expr: 90 - qa
    prof:
      expr: (80 - qa) * qa
sources:
    - id: cournot1838
      title: Recherches sur les principes mathématiques de la théorie des richesses
      authors: Antoine Augustin Cournot
      year: 1838
      finding: 'Analysed rival producers of an identical good who each choose a quantity, the price set by total output: the Cournot equilibrium, with price above cost.'
    - id: ks1983
      title: Quantity precommitment and Bertrand competition yield Cournot outcomes
      authors: David M. Kreps and José A. Scheinkman
      year: 1983
      finding: 'Bell Journal of Economics 14(2): when firms first choose capacities and then compete on price, the equilibrium is the Cournot outcome.'

    axes axes x_label "Crates landed (Q)" y_label "Auction price ($)" cross corner ticks.x [20,40,60,80,100] ticks.y [10,50,70,130] hidden true
    curve MC #2f6b8f expr "10 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 126 hidden true
    label label_MC "MC $10" right #2f6b8f attach_to MC hidden true
    curve D #3d3833 expr "130 - x" x_from 0 x_to 124 hidden true
    label label_D "D" right attach_to D hidden true
    curve segB #2f6b8f expr "4 + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 40 style.stroke_width 8 hidden true
    text label_segB "B: 40" #2f6b8f at.data [20,19] font_size 26 hidden true
    curve segA #b5482e expr "4 + 0*x" x_from 40 x_to 100 bind.x_to "40 + qa" style.stroke_width 8 hidden true
    text label_segA "A: {qa:0}" #b5482e at.data [70,19] font_size 26 bind {"at.data.0":"40 + qa/2"} hidden true
    curve pline dashed #8f887c expr "90 - qa + 0*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 bind.x_to "40 + qa" hidden true
    dot pt #b5482e at.on D at.x 100 bind {"at.x":"40 + qa"} hidden true
    region profit x_from 40 x_to 100 between ["pline","MC"] bind.x_to "40 + qa" style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text readout "Price ${P:0}\nA earns ${prof:0,}" at.data [102,118] font_size 32 hidden true
    portrait cournot cites ["cournot1838"] x 560 y 560 width 170 of "Antoine Augustin Cournot" hidden true
    dot p_mono #3d3833 at.on D at.x 60 hidden true
    label label_mono "one boat $70" right attach_to p_mono hidden true
    dot p_cour #b5482e at.on D at.x 80 hidden true
    label label_cour "catch $50" right #b5482e attach_to p_cour hidden true
    dot p_bert #2f6b8f at.on D at.x 120 hidden true
    label label_bert "price war $10" above #2f6b8f attach_to p_bert hidden true
    text ks "capacity first → Cournot" at.data [92,118] cites ["ks1983"] font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"\\text{A earns } (130 - q_A - q_B - 10)\\, q_A"},{"tex":"\\text{best: } q_A = (120 - q_B) \\div 2"},{"tex":"\\text{both alike: } q = 40,\\ P = \\$50"}] x 730 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Compete on catch instead"

Posting prices drove the quay all the way d (@draw MC label_MC@)own to cost. Change one thing: each boat must decide how many crates to land before the auction, and the auction sets the price.
    draw axes

B: The more fish come in, the lower the auction goes. Say, a hundred and thirty dollars minus a dollar for every crate landed.
    draw D label_D

Then why doesn't A just land a flood of fish and take the market?
    draw segB label_segB segA label_segA pt pline

B: Because every extra crate pulls down the price of all A's other crates (@animate qa 40 duration 3@) too. If B lands forty, A does best landing forty as well.
    draw profit readout

B: Eighty crates in all: the auction pays fifty dollars, and each boat earns sixteen hundred a day.
    highlight pt readout

And neither wants to change its catch. That rest (@draw cournot@)ing point is named after Antoine Augustin Cournot, who drew it in 1838.

    erase cournot

Try it: drag A's catch, with B's forty fixed, and find where A's profit peaks.
    explore params ["qa"]

    erase profit readout segA segB label_segB pline pt label_segA

B: So one boa (@draw p_mono label_mono@)t gets seventy dollars, two landi (@draw p_cour label_cour@)ng catches get fifty, and two post (@draw p_bert label_bert@)ing prices get ten.

(@highlight p_bert label_bert@) Under prices, a one-cent cut steals the whole quay. Under quant (@highlight p_cour label_cour@)ities, an extra crate mostly spoils your own price.

B: In 1983, David Kreps and José Scheinkman showed which world you're in: when capacity is f (@draw ks@)ixed first and prices come after, you get Cournot's answer.

    erase ks

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see where forty comes from?

@math

    erase mathq

A's profit is the price, a hundred and thirty minus both catches, less ten dollars of cost, times A's own crates.
    draw math_box math_line_1

It peaks when A lands half of what's left: a hundred and twenty minus B's catch, divided by two.
    draw math_line_2

If both boats think alike, each lands forty, and the price is fifty dollars.
    draw math_line_3

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

## Why not just agree?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Day 1
      - Day 2
      - Day 3
      - Day 4
      - Day 5
    values:
      - [1800, 1800, 1800, 1800, 1800]
      - [2025, 1800, 1800, 1800, 1800]
      - [2025, 1600, 1600, 1600, 1600]
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    y_label: A earns ($ a day)
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 2200
    reference:
      value: 1800
    box:
      x: 90
      'y': 150
      w: 640
      h: 490
sources:
    - id: doj_leniency
      title: 'Fighting cartels: why and how? (speech); Status report: Corporate Leniency Program'
      authors: US Department of Justice, Antitrust Division
      year: 1998
      finding: Under the 1978 leniency policy the Division received about one application a year; after the August 1993 revision, applications arrived at a rate closer to one a month.
      url: https://www.justice.gov/atr/speech/fighting-cartels-why-and-how-lessons-common-detecting-and-deterring-cartel-activity

    text deal "the deal\n$1,800" x 835 y 520 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t225 "+$225 once" #b5482e x 860 y 640 font_size 32 hidden true
    text t200 "−$200 every day" #2f6b8f x 860 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    text len "1993: immunity\nfor the first\nto confess" x 860 y 370 font_size 30 hidden true
    text len2 "~1 a year\n→ ~1 a month" #b5482e cites ["doj_leniency"] x 860 y 240 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Why not just agree?"

B: Fifty dollars beats ten, but one boat alone got seventy. So why don't the two simply agree to act like one?
    draw axes

That's a cartel. Each lands thirty crates instead of forty, (@draw reference_1 deal@) the auction pays seventy dollars, and each earns eighteen hundred a day.

Here are A's days if both keep the deal: eighteen hundred, every day.
    draw bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5

B: But the deal is just a promise. What if A quietly lands forty-five while B sticks to thirty?
    highlight bar_1

    quiz "Keep the deal, or land 45 crates?" judge false
        intro: You're boat A. Keep the deal, or land forty-five?
        right: Let's see what each choice pays.
        * Keep the deal
        * Land 45 crates

Cheating pays two thousand and twenty-five dollars on day one: two  (@draw t225@)hundred and twenty-five more.
    animate stage 1 duration 1.5

B: Then B notices, and both go back to forty crates: sixteen hundred a day,  (@draw t200@)two hundred less than the deal, every day after.
    animate stage 2 duration 2

So a cartel holds when tomorrow matters more than today's grab: firms meet again and again, and cheating is seen fast.
    highlight bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5

    erase t225 t200

B: It breaks when cheating hides: secret discounts, many firms, rare big orders, or a slump that looks like cheating.
    highlight bar_1

In 1993 the United States added a breaker: immunity for the first cartel member to confess. Applica (@draw len2@)tions went from about one a year to about one a month.
    draw len

    erase len len2 axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 reference_1 deal

    cards sorter size 1.3 check each
        Helps it hold: Few firms, Prices seen at once
        Helps it break: Secret discounts, A slump in demand, Easy entry, Leniency for confessing

    ask "Which of these help a cartel hold, and which help it break? Sort each card." on sorter store f
        intro: Sort them: which of these help a cartel hold, and which help it break?
        right: All {f} right: you can read a cartel's odds.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try.

## How cartels really die
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: OPEC would not want you at the table.
    good: You'd spot a cartel cracking before most.
    poor: Cartels count on people missing these.
    none: 'No answers: the quiet kind of collusion.'
use: timeline
with:
    view:
      from: 1988
      to: 2000
    eras:
      - {from: 1990, to: 1999, label: vitamin cartel, color: '#b5482e'}
    events:
      - {id: start, date: 1990, label: 'Roche, BASF,', sublabel: Rhône-Poulenc, priority: 1, cites: [doj_vit]}
      - {id: len, date: 1993, label: US leniency rule, priority: 1, cites: [doj_leniency]}
      - {id: fines, date: 1999-05, label: Fines, sublabel: $500m + $225m, priority: 1, emphasize: true, cites: [doj_vit]}
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 390
      w: 880
      h: 250
sources:
    - id: doj_vit
      title: F. Hoffmann-La Roche and BASF agree to pay record criminal fines for participating in international vitamin cartel (press release, 20 May 1999)
      authors: US Department of Justice
      year: 1999
      finding: Roche agreed to pay $500 million, then the highest criminal fine ever, and BASF $225 million; Rhône-Poulenc was not fined because it cooperated under the amnesty programme. The conspiracy ran from about January 1990 to February 1999 (the European Commission dates vitamins A and E from September 1989).
      url: https://www.justice.gov/archive/opa/pr/1999/May/196at.htm
    - id: doj_leniency
      title: 'Fighting cartels: why and how? (speech)'
      authors: US Department of Justice, Antitrust Division
      year: 1998
      finding: After the August 1993 revision of the corporate leniency policy, applications rose from about one a year to about one a month.
      url: https://www.justice.gov/atr/speech/fighting-cartels-why-and-how-lessons-common-detecting-and-deterring-cartel-activity
    - id: ls2006
      title: What Determines Cartel Success?
      authors: Margaret C. Levenstein and Valerie Y. Suslow
      year: 2006
      finding: 'Journal of Economic Literature 44(1): average cartel duration across studies is about five years, many break up within a year; cheating breaks cartels occasionally, but the biggest challenges are entry and adjusting the agreement to changing conditions.'
    - id: dg2000
      title: 'Price competition and market concentration: an experimental study'
      authors: Martin Dufwenberg and Uri Gneezy
      year: 2000
      finding: 'International Journal of Industrial Organization 18: with two competitors, prices stayed well above the Bertrand prediction; with three or four, after some learning, Bertrand predicted well.'

    text conf "Rhône-Poulenc:\nconfessed, no fine" #2f6b8f cites ["doj_vit"] x 800 y 300 font_size 30 hidden true
    text myth "cheating" x 200 y 290 font_size 34 hidden true
    mark strike around myth kind strike hidden true
    text entry "entry,\nrenegotiation" #2f6b8f cites ["ls2006"] x 480 y 290 font_size 32 hidden true
    text dur "≈ 5 years\non average" cites ["ls2006"] x 780 y 290 font_size 32 hidden true
    text stamp1 "Cartels break:\nmostly agreed" #3d6b3a x 300 y 280 font_size 32 hidden true
    text stamp2 "Two-firm price war:\ncontested" #b5482e cites ["dg2000"] x 700 y 280 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "How cartels really die"

The oil price collapse of 1986 is the famous broken cartel. Here's a quieter one: the vitamins added to food and animal feed.
    draw axis eras

B: From about 1990, Roche (@draw event_start@), BASF and Rhône-Poulenc fixed vitamin prices and split the world market between them.

It held for nearly a decade. No price war, no visible cheating.
    highlight eras

B: It ended b (@draw event_len@)ecause one member confessed: Rhône-Poulenc went (@draw conf@) to the US authorities under that amnesty rule.

In 1999 Roche paid (@draw event_fines@) a five-hundred-million-dollar fine in the US, then the largest criminal fine ever, and BASF two hundred and twenty-five million. Rhône-Poulenc paid nothing.

    erase conf

B: So this one didn' (@draw myth@)t die of cheating either. Margaret Levenstein and Valerie Suslow surveyed the studies of many cartels: cheating broke some (@draw strike@), but it wasn't the biggest threat.

Entry w (@draw entry@)as, and quarrels over redrawing the deal when conditions change: high prices invite newcomers, and members fight over new shares.

B: And they last: about five years on average, though many fall apart within a year.
    draw dur

    erase myth strike entry dur

So cartels  (@draw stamp1@)can break: mostly agreed. Why they break, and how many are never caught, is still being worked out.

Bertrand's two- (@draw stamp2@)firm price war is shakier. In a lab experiment, two price-setters stayed well above cost; with three or four, prices did fall towards it.

    pause 0.4

    erase axis eras events stamp1 stamp2

    row recap gap 40 fit.h 260 fit.w 840 fit.x 80 fit.y 250
        box r1 "Prices posted\n→ cost" radius 12 font_size 28
        box r2 "Capacity fixed\n→ in between" radius 12 font_size 28
        box r3 "Cartel → near\nmonopoly, while\ncheats are seen" radius 12 font_size 28
    parallel true

    quiz "Two online shops sell the identical charger. Shoppers compare prices on one site, and either shop can ship to everyone. Where do prices go?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Close to cost: a cent cheaper wins every shopper, so each keeps undercutting the other.
        wrong: If one shop is a cent cheaper, how many shoppers does the other keep? None: so prices go close to cost.
        + Close to cost
        * Halfway to the monopoly price
        * The monopoly price

    quiz "Two cement firms each built one kiln that took years to build and now runs flat out. Their price is most likely…"
        right: Above cost but below monopoly: capacity was fixed first, so they compete on quantity, Cournot-style.
        wrong: Can either firm serve the whole market by undercutting? No: so the price stays above cost, below monopoly.
        * Close to cost
        + Above cost but below the monopoly price
        * The monopoly price

    quiz "Which cartel is most likely to last?"
        right: The salt makers: few firms, prices seen every week, so a cheat is caught at once and gains little.
        wrong: Ask how soon a cheat would be seen: among the salt makers, within a week. That cartel lasts.
        + Three salt makers with public weekly price lists, selling to many small shops
        * Twenty builders bidding in secret for one big bridge contract a decade

B: So two firms can mean a price war or quiet collusion. What decides it is what they compete with, how often they meet, and whether anyone can see a cheat.
    highlight r1 r2 r3

